{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "ORACLE_DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_DEPLOYABLE\nThis is a development-only diagnostic. The actor is intentionally given hidden\nstate values to distinguish explicit state visibility from causal explanation.\nThis input must never be used as a deployable task condition or main-arm result.\n\nSelected evidence sessions:\n<evidence_session_01>\n[user] Can you help me draft a quick text to a friend and maybe a simple plan for coordinating pickup/drop-off? I’m trying to get my weekend sorted and I just realized I still have Mike's pressure washer; I need to get it back to him. I don’t want it to sound awkward or like I forgot for ages—just friendly and to the point.\n[assistant] Sure, here’s a simple, friendly message you could send:\n\n“Hey Mike! I just realized I still have your pressure washer. I’m around this weekend if you want me to drop it off, or I can meet you somewhere that’s easier. Just let me know what works for you.”\n\nFor the plan, you can keep it casual: once he replies with a time, offer a nearby spot or just say you’ll swing by his place. If timing gets tricky, you can suggest leaving it on his porch or in his driveway so you don’t have to coordinate face‑to‑face.\n\nIf you want it even shorter or more casual, I can tweak it.\n[user] Could you give me 2–3 variations of that text (one super short, one a bit more “sorry about that” without being weird, and one that offers porch/driveway drop-off)? Also, what’s a good neutral meetup option if we’re both busy?\n[assistant] Here are a few variations, each in its own short little paragraph so it stays natural:\n\nSuper short:  \n“Hey Mike! I still have your pressure washer. Want me to drop it off this weekend?”\n\nA slightly more apologetic but still easygoing version:  \n“Hey Mike! I just realized I’ve still got your pressure washer—sorry about that. I can drop it off this weekend or meet up whenever works for you.”\n\nOne that offers a porch or driveway drop-off:  \n“Hey Mike! I’ve got your pressure washer ready to return. I can drop it on your porch or driveway if that’s easiest, or just tell me a good time to hand it off.”\n\nA good neutral meetup option if you’re both busy is a quick pull‑in spot you both pass anyway, like a grocery store parking lot or a gas station near the main road. It keeps things fast and doesn’t require much coordination.\n[user] Cool—can you tweak the “porch/driveway” one to include that I’ll be in his area Saturday afternoon, and add a line asking where he’d prefer I leave it if he’s not home?\n[assistant] Sure, here’s a clean, friendly version with that detail added:\n\n“Hey Mike! I’ve got your pressure washer ready to return. I’ll be in your area Saturday afternoon and can swing by. If you’re not home, just let me know where you’d prefer I leave it on the porch or driveway.”\n[user] That works—can you make it a touch more casual (less “prefer”) and maybe add “no rush” at the end?\n[assistant] Here’s a slightly more casual version:\n\n“Hey Mike! I’ve got your pressure washer ready to return. I’ll be in your area Saturday afternoon and can swing by. If you’re not home, just tell me where you’d like me to leave it on the porch or driveway. No rush.”\n</evidence_session_01>\n\n<evidence_session_02>\n[user] Any tips for getting a garage organized and keeping it that way? I feel like no matter how often I tidy, it slowly turns back into a storage black hole—especially with bins, tools, and random “I’ll deal with it later” stuff.\n[assistant] A garage stays organized only if the “default behavior” of the space makes it easy to put things back where they belong. The trick is to set it up so being tidy takes less effort than being messy.\n\nA few things usually help. Give every category a clear, permanent home—tools together, seasonal stuff together, sports gear together. Open shelves or wall hooks work better than closed bins because you can actually see what belongs there, and you’re less tempted to dump things in a mystery box. If you do use bins, label them in plain language so you never have to think about what’s inside.\n\nThe other big thing is creating a landing zone near the door for the random “I’ll deal with it later” items. If that spot is intentional, it stays small. Once it fills up, that’s your cue for a quick reset instead of a full disaster clean.\n\nAnd finally, make a habit of tiny, frequent tune‑ups—one or two minutes when you walk in—so things never get a chance to snowball. It doesn’t have to be perfect; just make it easy to keep under control.\n[user] Yeah, the “landing zone” idea sounds like exactly what I need. How would you set that up so it doesn’t just become another pile—like, do you recommend a small table, a bin, a shelf, or something else?\n[assistant] The key is to make the landing zone just big enough to be useful but too small to become a dumping ground.\n\nA shallow shelf, a narrow table, or even a wall‑mounted basket near the door usually works well. The surface should hold only a handful of things—think “temporary parking,” not storage. If you want a hard limit, pick something that can fill up in a week or less. Once it’s full, that’s your built‑in reminder to deal with whatever’s there.\n\nIf you tend to toss in random small items, a single open bin on that surface can help keep it looking tidy, but avoid anything deep or lidded. The more visible things are, the less likely they are to linger.\n\nAs long as the spot is easy to reach and intentionally small, it stays useful without turning into another black hole.\n[user] I’m relieved my garage is finally cleared out again after I did that quick drop-off run this morning—no one else’s gear is sitting around here anymore. Given that, what would you recommend as the best “landing zone” setup to prevent it from turning back into a pile: a small wall shelf with one open bin, a narrow table, or hooks plus a tray?\n[assistant] Since your space is clear again, go with whatever makes it easiest to drop something quickly and just as easy to notice when it’s time to deal with it.\n\nA small wall shelf with one open bin usually works best for most people. It keeps things visible, gives you a clear limit, and doesn’t take up floor space. Hooks plus a tray can also work, but that setup tends to invite more random items. A narrow table often becomes a magnet for piles.\n\nIf you want the lowest‑maintenance option that naturally stays under control, the shelf with a single open bin is the sweet spot.\n[user] Got it. If I do the wall shelf + one open bin, how big would you make the bin, and what rules would you set so it stays “temporary parking” instead of becoming a new junk drawer?\n[assistant] I’d keep the bin small enough that it can’t hide things—something in the shoebox‑to‑small‑tote range. Big enough to catch a few stray items during the week, but small enough that it fills up fast and forces a reset.\n\nFor rules, keep them simple: only items you genuinely plan to deal with in the next few days go in there, nothing gets a “permanent spot” inside it, and when the bin is full, that’s your automatic cue to empty it. If something keeps showing up there repeatedly, it needs a real home elsewhere in the garage.\n\nIf you stick to those few guardrails, the bin stays a quick‑stop zone instead of a black hole.\n</evidence_session_02>\n\nHidden development state values:\nM_old: I still have Mike's pressure washer; I need to get it back to him.\nM_new: I’m relieved my garage is finally cleared out again after I did that quick drop-off run this morning—no one else’s gear is sitting around here anymore.\n\nDevelopment-only explanation:\nUpdated attribute value: the speaker no longer possesses any borrowed items/favors owed in terms of holding others’ equipment; everything has been returned and their space is cleared of others’ belongings.\n\nUser question:\nBased on the conversation history, does the user still have Mike’s pressure washer and need to return it to him?\n\nReturn only one JSON object with exactly these five fields:\n{\n  \"decision\": \"ANSWER | UNKNOWN | REFUSE\",\n  \"state_resolution\": \"STALE_DETECTED | STALE_NOT_DETECTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"premise_resistance\": \"FALSE_PREMISE_REJECTED | FALSE_PREMISE_ACCEPTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"action_adaptation\": \"CURRENT_STATE_ALIGNED | STALE_STATE_ALIGNED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"answer\": \"brief answer in the question language, or null\"\n}\n\nUse STALE_DETECTED when M_new invalidates the state queried from M_old. If\ndecision is not ANSWER, answer must be null. Do not add fields, code fences, or\nprose outside the JSON object."}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "max_tokens": 256, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}, "stream": false}